/-!
# The bilinear Hardy--Littlewood--Sobolev inequality on the line

For `0 < λ < 1` and `r = 2 / (2 - λ)` the Riesz kernel `|s - u|^{-λ}` defines a bounded bilinear
form on `L^r(ℝ) × L^r(ℝ)`:

`∫∫ |s - u|^{-λ} h(s) g(u) ds du ≤ c_λ ‖h‖_r ‖g‖_r`.

Note `1/r + 1/r + λ = 2`, the homogeneity forced by scaling, so this is the symmetric endpoint of
the Hardy--Littlewood--Sobolev inequality.  Everything is stated with `lintegral`s and
`ℝ≥0∞`-valued functions, so no integrability hypotheses are needed.

## Method

The proof is the classical level-set argument, which needs no interpolation theorem.  Writing
`A_m = {2^m ≤ h < 2^{m+1}}` and `B_n = {2^n ≤ g < 2^{n+1}}` one has `h ≤ ∑_m 2^{m+1} 1_{A_m}`, and
the two "bathtub" bounds

`∫∫_{A × B} |s-u|^{-λ} ≤ c_λ |A| |B|^{1-λ}`,  `∫∫_{A × B} |s-u|^{-λ} ≤ c_λ |B| |A|^{1-λ}`

turn the double sum into `∑_{m,n} 2^{-(r-1)|m-n|}(x_m + y_n)` where `x_m = 2^{mr}|A_m|` and
`y_n = 2^{nr}|B_n|` satisfy `∑_m x_m ≤ ‖h‖_r^r` and `∑_n y_n ≤ ‖g‖_r^r`.  The geometric factor is
summable precisely because `r > 1`.

## Main result

* `Auto.HardyLittlewoodSobolev.lintegral_bilinear_riesz_le`

## References

* E. M. Stein, *Singular integrals and differentiability properties of functions*, Ch. V.
* E. H. Lieb and M. Loss, *Analysis*, Theorem 4.3.
-/

